      <!-- RIGHT: ARTICLE CONTENT -->
      <article style="min-width: 0; max-width: 720px; font-family: 'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif; font-size: 17px; line-height: 1.72; color: #0A0A0A;">

        <!-- INTRO -->
        <p style="margin-bottom: 1.4em;">A <strong>compressed gas aerosol</strong> uses a non-condensable gas — most commonly nitrogen or carbon dioxide — stored entirely in the vapour phase at high initial pressure. Unlike systems that rely on a liquefied propellant, the pressure inside a compressed gas aerosol decreases progressively with each actuation because there is no liquid reservoir to replenish expelled gas. This fundamental distinction governs every aspect of spray performance, formulation compatibility, and storage behaviour, and it is the first engineering decision any aerosol product developer must make. Understanding how each propellant system behaves across its pressure-temperature envelope is essential for designing a product that performs consistently, complies with applicable regulations, and reaches market with predictable shelf life.</p>

        <!-- SECTION 1 -->
        <section id="section-1">
          <h2 style="font-family: 'Playfair Display', Georgia, serif; font-size: clamp(1.25rem, 2.5vw, 1.6rem); font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: -0.01em; margin: 2.4em 0 0.6em; padding-top: 0.4em;">What Is a Compressed Gas Aerosol?</h2>
          <p style="margin-bottom: 1.4em;">A compressed gas aerosol stores its propellant entirely as a gas within the can headspace, with no phase change occurring during storage or use. The gas is charged at a defined pressure at the point of filling, and this charge represents the system's entire pressure reservoir. Common compressed gas propellants include nitrogen (N₂), carbon dioxide (CO₂), and compressed air in certain specialised applications. Each is stored in its gaseous state at ambient temperatures, meaning there is no liquid propellant phase present inside the can at any point in the product's life cycle.</p>

          <p style="margin-bottom: 1.4em;">The operating principle is straightforward but has significant implications: as product is dispensed and the headspace volume expands, gas pressure drops according to <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ideal_gas_law" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="color: #0968e5;">ideal gas law behaviour</a>. The relationship between volume and pressure means that a can at 50% fill will deliver noticeably lower spray velocity than it did when full, all else being equal. This pressure decay is not a defect — it is an intrinsic characteristic of the technology that formulators must account for in product design.</p>

          <h3 style="font-family: 'Playfair Display', Georgia, serif; font-size: 1.15rem; font-weight: 600; margin: 1.8em 0 0.5em;">Nitrogen as a Compressed Gas Propellant</h3>
          <p style="margin-bottom: 1.4em;">Nitrogen is the most widely used compressed gas propellant due to its chemical inertness, lack of global warming potential (GWP = 0), non-flammability, and complete absence of solvency effects on organic formulations. It is the standard choice for food-contact aerosols, pharmaceutical applications, and products where hydrocarbon solvency would compromise formulation stability. As documented by <a href="https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/Nitrogen" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="color: #0968e5;">PubChem</a>, nitrogen is a diatomic gas with near-ideal behaviour across the pressure and temperature ranges relevant to aerosol applications.</p>

          <h3 style="font-family: 'Playfair Display', Georgia, serif; font-size: 1.15rem; font-weight: 600; margin: 1.8em 0 0.5em;">Carbon Dioxide as a Compressed Gas Propellant</h3>
          <p style="margin-bottom: 1.4em;">Carbon dioxide occupies a unique position: at the pressures and temperatures encountered in aerosol manufacturing, CO₂ may exist at or near its critical point, exhibiting more complex phase behaviour than nitrogen. It is moderately soluble in many organic solvents and aqueous systems, which means dissolved CO₂ can influence formulation pH and contribute additional pressure as it evolves from solution. CO₂ is widely used in food aerosol applications — particularly beverage and whipped cream systems — where its interaction with the product is by design rather than a liability.</p>

          <ul style="padding-left: 1.5em; margin-bottom: 1.4em;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 0.4em;">Nitrogen: GWP = 0, non-flammable, inert, no solvency — preferred for food, pharma, and sensitive formulations</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 0.4em;">CO₂: Moderate solubility in water and organics, can affect pH, higher initial pressure potential</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 0.4em;">Compressed air: Limited industrial use; oxygen content creates oxidation and flammability risks in many formulations</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 0.4em;">Filling is performed at a defined charge pressure; no liquid propellant phase is introduced to the can</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <!-- DIAGRAM IMAGE -->
        <img
          src={diagramUrl}
          alt="compressed gas aerosol versus liquefied gas phase comparison in glass laboratory vessels — Global Aerosols diagram"
          width="900" height="500"
